WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.600
NumPy.

2
00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:05.233
Every shape error you'll ever debug in deep learning starts here.

3
00:00:06.433 --> 00:00:09.933
A NumPy array is a typed, contiguous block of memory.

4
00:00:10.233 --> 00:00:16.833
Operations on it run as compiled C loops, routinely ten to a hundred times faster than Python loops.

5
00:00:17.133 --> 00:00:20.038
The mental shift is to think in whole arrays.

6
00:00:20.038 --> 00:00:25.267
Dividing one array by another handles every pair at once, with no index in sight.

7
00:00:26.467 --> 00:00:31.167
The concept to truly master is broadcasting: how mismatched shapes stretch to fit.

8
00:00:31.467 --> 00:00:33.533
First, align the shapes from the right.

9
00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:38.633
Each dimension must be equal, or one, which stretches, or missing, which is added.

10
00:00:38.933 --> 00:00:41.100
If neither holds, you get a shape error.

11
00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:43.533
Let's watch it happen.

12
00:00:43.833 --> 00:00:45.982
This column has shape three by one.

13
00:00:45.982 --> 00:00:47.333
It stretches sideways.

14
00:00:47.633 --> 00:00:49.525
This row has shape one by four.

15
00:00:49.525 --> 00:00:50.867
It stretches downward.

16
00:00:51.167 --> 00:00:54.200
They meet at three by four, with every pairing computed.

17
00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:56.600
Here it is in code.

18
00:00:56.900 --> 00:01:00.400
Two arrays hold asteroid diameters and miss distances.

19
00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.333
One division handles all four pairs at once.

20
00:01:03.633 --> 00:01:08.233
And a column plus a row broadcasts to shape three by four, just as we drew.

21
00:01:09.433 --> 00:01:10.400
A real case.

22
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:16.533
Mission Control RAG keeps its chunk embeddings in a ten thousand by fifteen thirty six matrix.

23
00:01:16.833 --> 00:01:21.832
Scoring a query against the whole corpus is one matrix multiply, milliseconds.

24
00:01:21.832 --> 00:01:25.100
The loop version is about two hundred times slower.

25
00:01:25.400 --> 00:01:27.990
And normalizing the rows is broadcasting.

26
00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:32.033
This is vector search before you ever install a vector database.

27
00:01:33.233 --> 00:01:34.117
Let's recap.

28
00:01:34.117 --> 00:01:36.400
Arrays run as compiled C loops.

29
00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:38.467
Think in whole arrays, not loops.

30
00:01:38.767 --> 00:01:41.067
Broadcasting aligns shapes from the right.

31
00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:44.476
And PyTorch follows exactly the same rules.

32
00:01:44.476 --> 00:01:45.633
Next up: pandas.
